<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elena Ceaușescu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elena Ceaușescu (1916–1989) was the wife of the Romanian communist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a> and from the 1970s the second most powerful person in the country, as a member of the party's top body and first deputy prime minister. Despite little formal schooling she was promoted as a world-famous chemist. She was tried with her husband by a military tribunal and executed with him on 25 December 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elena-ceausescu.jpg" alt="Elena Ceaușescu" width="330" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: not credited · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elena_Ceausescu_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elena Ceaușescu was born Lenuța Petrescu into a peasant family in Petrești commune, Dâmbovița County, in Wallachia. Her father was a ploughman, and she received only an elementary education. She moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> with her brother and worked as a laboratory assistant and then in a textile factory. In 1939 she joined the Bucharest branch of the Romanian Communist Party and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a>, whom she married in December 1947. She obtained a degree in chemical engineering in 1957 and a doctorate in 1967, though there is anecdotal evidence that her thesis was written by a university professor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Communists took power she worked as a secretary in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and was an unimportant figure until her husband became General Secretary of the party. On a state visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in June 1971 she noted how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-qing">Jiang Qing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>'s wife, held a position of power, and she then began her own political rise.</p><p>She became a full member of the Central Committee in July 1972 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in June 1973, the second most influential person after her husband. She was elected to the Great National Assembly in March 1975, for Pitești, and in March 1980 became First Deputy Prime Minister, a post she kept until her death.</p><p>From the early 1980s she was the object of a cult of personality as intense as her husband's, which called her the Mother of the Nation. In science she was hired at the National Institute for Chemical Research in 1957 and became a member of the Romanian Academy's section for chemical sciences in 1974, and in 1978 the Royal Institute of Chemistry admitted her as a Fellow. After 1989 several scientists claimed she had forced them to write papers in her name and to share credit on patents.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She fled with her husband on 22 December 1989 after the events in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Timișoara</a> led to the Romanian Revolution, and they were captured at Târgoviște. At their trial she answered only a few questions. On the afternoon of 25 December 1989 they were turned over to a firing squad and executed, with their arms tied behind their backs. She was the only woman ever executed by the modern state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. She was survived by her mother, her brother Gheorghe Petrescu and three children, Valentin, Zoia and Nicu, and was buried in Ghencea Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elena Ceaușescu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army turned against Ceaușescu, who was captured and executed with his wife <b>Elena</b> on 25 December 1989 after a summary trial.</blockquote><a href="/state/socialist-republic-of-romania">Socialist Republic of Romania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Petrești, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Târgoviște, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.65,25.33&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Petrești on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Elena Ceaușescu"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4716 3755h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4716 3755h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.65,25.33&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
